A = {
"jp-1842": dict(
 bluf="China's defeat in the Opium War convinced Japan's rulers that firing on Western ships invited disaster. Japan softened its policy and began studying Western military technology, an early sign of the pragmatism that would drive its modernization.",
 lens="Watching a neighbour's defeat can teach more than one's own experience."),
"eu184570": dict(
 bluf="The Great Famine killed about a million people and drove another million to emigrate, cutting Ireland's population by a quarter. British policies that kept food exports flowing and limited relief turned a crop failure into a catastrophe, fuelling Irish nationalism and building a global Irish diaspora.",
 why="Over a third of the population depended almost entirely on the potato, grown on tiny plots rented from often absentee landlords, and blight destroyed the crop repeatedly from 1845. The British government, committed to free-market principles, limited relief and let food exports continue.",
 then="Ireland's population fell by about a quarter and kept shrinking until the 20th century, as mass emigration to the United States, Britain and Australia continued. Bitterness toward British rule strengthened nationalist movements.",
 lens="Famines are rarely just about food shortage; they are about who has access to food and who decides."),
"na184537": dict(
 bluf="Annexing Texas brought a large slave state into the Union and made war with Mexico likely. 'Manifest Destiny' gave expansion an ideological justification that shaped US policy for a century.",
 lens="Ideologies of destiny turn ambitions into perceived rights."),
"oc-nzwars": dict(
 bluf="The New Zealand Wars were fought over land and sovereignty, and ended with the colonial government confiscating millions of acres of Māori land. They decided that settlers, not Māori, would control New Zealand.",
 lens="Wars over land often end in confiscation dressed up as punishment."),
"na184638": dict(
 bluf="The United States took about half of Mexico's territory, including California and the Southwest, in a war many Americans at the time saw as aggression. The land made the United States a continental, Pacific power, but the fight over whether slavery could spread into it drove the country toward civil war.",
 story="Mexico had never accepted Texas's independence or its US annexation, and the two countries disputed the border. President Polk sent troops into the disputed zone, and after a clash in April 1846 he declared that Mexico had 'shed American blood upon American soil'. US forces took California and New Mexico and, after landing at Veracruz, captured Mexico City in September 1847.",
 why="Polk wanted California and its Pacific ports, many Americans believed in 'Manifest Destiny', and Mexico, politically unstable, refused to sell.",
 trig="A clash between US and Mexican troops in the disputed land north of the Rio Grande in April 1846.",
 then="Mexico gave up about half its territory in the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo (1848). The fight over allowing slavery in the new lands split the parties along sectional lines. Many Civil War generals, including Grant and Lee, first fought here.",
 now="California, Texas and the Southwest's Mexican heritage come from this war, and Mexico's memory of 'the US intervention' still shapes its wariness of its northern neighbour.",
 lens="Territorial gains can destabilize the winner: the new land raised the question that tore the United States apart."),
"d-cornlaws": dict(
 bluf="Repealing the Corn Laws marked Britain's shift from protecting landowners to free trade serving industry and urban consumers. It made Britain the champion of free trade for 80 years and split the Conservative Party.",
 lens="Trade policy is a contest between interest groups: ask who gains from protection and who pays for it."),
"la184822": dict(
 bluf="The treaty transferred about half of Mexico's territory, including California, to the United States, and made tens of thousands of Mexicans US residents overnight. It fixed much of the modern US-Mexico border and created the Mexican-American population of the Southwest.",
 lens="Borders move across people as well as people across borders."),
"na184839": dict(
 bluf="The Gold Rush brought a sudden flood of migrants from around the world, made California a state within two years and tied the Pacific coast to the US economy. For Native Californians it brought genocidal violence that destroyed most of their population.",
 then="California became a state in 1850. Its Native population fell from about 150,000 to about 30,000 by 1870 through killings, disease and starvation, which California's governor formally acknowledged as genocide in 2019.",
 lens="Resource rushes bring rapid growth, and often catastrophic violence against whoever is already there."),
"na184840": dict(
 bluf="Seneca Falls launched the organized women's rights movement in the United States, demanding equality including the vote. It used the language of the Declaration of Independence to expose that document's exclusions.",
 lens="Movements gain power by holding a society to its own founding principles."),
"eu184871": dict(
 bluf="The revolutions of 1848 were Europe's first continent-wide wave of revolt, demanding constitutions, national unity and social reform. Most were crushed within a year, but they ended serfdom in Central Europe, made nationalism the dominant political force, and showed that liberal revolution alone could not unify Germany or Italy.",
 why="Bad harvests, including potato blight, an economic slump and unemployment, on top of decades of liberal and nationalist frustration with the conservative order set up in 1815.",
 trig="The February revolution in Paris, which overthrew King Louis-Philippe.",
 then="Austria abolished serfdom, Prussia got a constitution, and France got universal male suffrage, then elected Louis-Napoleon, who made himself emperor. Conservative forces regrouped, and unification came later through Prussian and Piedmontese power politics rather than liberal parliaments.",
 now="'1848' is the template for sudden waves of revolt, such as 1989 or the Arab Spring.",
 lens="Revolutionary waves spread fast across connected societies but fail when armies stay loyal to rulers."),
"eu184871b": dict(
 bluf="The Communist Manifesto framed history as class struggle and predicted that industrial workers would overthrow capitalism. Marx's ideas became the doctrine of mass socialist parties and, in the 20th century, of revolutionary states that ruled a third of humanity.",
 why="Industrialization had created a new urban working class in harsh conditions, capitalism's booms and busts were plain to see, and the revolutionary climate of 1848 made radical change seem possible.",
 then="Marx's later work, above all Das Kapital (1867), shaped socialist and labour movements. The Russian Revolution of 1917 put Marxists in power, while reformist socialists turned to democratic politics and the welfare state.",
 now="Regimes claiming Marx's inspiration once ruled a third of humanity, and the Manifesto remains one of the most widely read political texts ever written.",
 lens="Ideas about inequality win mass followings when economic change produces visible winners and losers."),
"d-frankfurt": dict(
 bluf="The Frankfurt Parliament was Germany's first attempt at unification through a liberal, elected assembly. Its failure meant Germany would be united by Prussian military power instead, with lasting consequences for German democracy.",
 lens="Parliaments without armies depend on the rulers who have them."),
"as185030": dict(
 bluf="The Taiping Rebellion, led by a man who claimed to be Jesus's younger brother, was one of the deadliest wars in history, killing perhaps 20 to 30 million people. The Qing survived only by relying on regional armies and Western help, which permanently weakened central authority.",
 story="Hong Xiuquan, after failing the civil service exams, read Christian tracts and came to believe he was the younger brother of Jesus. His movement grew among the poor and the Hakka minority of southern China. In 1853 the Taiping took Nanjing, made it their 'Heavenly Capital' and ruled much of the Yangtze valley until Qing forces retook it in 1864.",
 why="Population pressure, poverty, Qing weakness after the Opium War, ethnic tensions, and a charismatic religious message promising equality.",
 then="The Qing relied on regional armies raised by Chinese officials such as Zeng Guofan and Li Hongzhang, plus Western-officered units; these regional power bases later fed warlordism. The devastation of the Yangtze valley set back China's economy.",
 now="It is one of the deadliest wars in history yet little known outside China. Both Nationalists and Communists later claimed the Taiping as revolutionary forerunners.",
 lens="When a state cannot crush a rebellion with its own forces, the regional armies it relies on become its next problem."),
"na185041": dict(
 bluf="The Compromise of 1850 admitted California as a free state and, in exchange, passed a harsh Fugitive Slave Act. Forcing Northerners to help return people who had escaped slavery radicalized Northern opinion against it.",
 lens="Concessions that make bystanders complicit can radicalize them."),
"oc-gold": dict(
 bluf="The gold rushes turned Australia from a penal colony into a booming immigrant society, quadrupling its population in twenty years. Hostility to Chinese miners also produced the first laws of what became the White Australia policy.",
 lens="Immigration booms often bring both prosperity and an exclusionary backlash."),
"as185331": dict(
 bluf="Perry's warships forced Japan to end two centuries of restricted foreign contact. The shock discredited the Tokugawa shogunate and set off the crisis that led to the Meiji Restoration and Japan's rapid transformation into a modern industrial and imperial power.",
 story="In July 1853 Commodore Matthew Perry sailed four ships, two of them steam-powered, into Edo Bay and delivered a letter demanding trade and good treatment for shipwrecked sailors. He promised to return the next year with a bigger fleet. Japan had no navy able to resist.",
 why="The United States wanted coaling stations for steamships on the route to China, protection for its whalers and new markets; China's defeat by Britain had shown how vulnerable Asian states were.",
 then="The Treaty of Kanagawa (1854) and the unequal treaties of 1858 followed. Anger at the shogunate's surrender fed a movement to restore the emperor, and the shogunate fell in 1868.",
 now="Japan's transformation into a modern power begins here.",
 lens="External shocks expose the weakness of old regimes and empower reformers who can explain how to respond."),
"eu185372": dict(
 bluf="Britain and France fought Russia to stop it from dominating the weakening Ottoman Empire and the Black Sea. Russia's defeat exposed its backwardness, prompting the emancipation of the serfs, and broke up the Concert of Europe, opening the way to the wars of Italian and German unification.",
 why="Russian ambitions in the Ottoman Balkans and the straits, a quarrel over the protection of Christians in the Holy Land, and British and French determination to keep Russia out of the Mediterranean.",
 trig="Russia's occupation of the Ottoman Danubian principalities (Moldavia and Wallachia) in 1853.",
 then="The Treaty of Paris (1856) banned warships from the Black Sea. A humiliated Russia freed its serfs and modernized its army. Austria's neutrality cost it Russia's friendship, leaving it isolated when Piedmont and Prussia went to war to unify Italy and Germany.",
 now="It was the first war covered by telegraph and photography. Crimea and Sevastopol remain central to Russian strategy, from 1854 to 2014.",
 lens="Great powers fight to stop a rival from gaining a decisive position as a declining empire weakens."),
"na185442": dict(
 bluf="The Kansas-Nebraska Act scrapped the Missouri Compromise line and let settlers vote on slavery, turning Kansas into a battleground. It destroyed the Whig Party, created the Republican Party and set the country on the path to war.",
 lens="Reopening a settled question can unleash the conflicts the original settlement contained."),
"jp-kanagawa": dict(
 bluf="The Treaty of Kanagawa was Japan's first treaty with a Western power, opening two ports and ending over 200 years of restricted contact. It set off the internal crisis that toppled the shogunate.",
 lens="A first concession under pressure invites the next."),
"oc-eureka": dict(
 bluf="The Eureka rebellion was a miners' revolt against taxation without representation that, though crushed, quickly won its demands. It is remembered as a founding moment of Australian democracy.",
 lens="Movements that lose the battle can still win the argument."),
"se-bowring": dict(
 bluf="King Mongkut accepted free trade and extraterritorial rights on British terms to avoid war, opening Siam to Western commerce. Strategic concessions and modernization let Siam keep its independence while its neighbours were colonized.",
 lens="Small states sometimes preserve their sovereignty by conceding on trade."),
"as185732": dict(
 bluf="The rebellion of 1857 was the largest challenge to British rule in India, combining a soldiers' mutiny with uprisings by princes, landlords and peasants. Its suppression ended East India Company rule and the Mughal dynasty, and hardened British and Indian attitudes for the rest of the Raj.",
 story="In May 1857 Indian soldiers (sepoys) at Meerut mutinied over new rifle cartridges rumoured to be greased with cow and pig fat, offensive to Hindus and Muslims alike. They marched to Delhi and proclaimed the aged Mughal emperor Bahadur Shah II their leader. Revolt spread across northern India, both sides committed atrocities, and British forces retook Delhi and Lucknow by 1858.",
 why="Annexations of Indian states such as Awadh (1856), lost status for landed elites, heavy land taxes, fears of forced Christianization and Westernization, and grievances among sepoys over pay and service overseas.",
 trig="The cartridge controversy and the harsh punishment of sepoys at Meerut who refused to use them.",
 then="Britain abolished the Company and ruled India directly through a viceroy; the last Mughal emperor was exiled to Burma. Britain stopped annexing princely states, recruited soldiers from groups it considered loyal, and kept itself more segregated from Indian society.",
 now="Indian nationalists call it the First War of Independence, and many historians trace British policies of treating Hindus and Muslims as separate blocs to this period.",
 lens="Rebellions often begin over a symbolic grievance that crystallizes deeper ones."),
"na185743": dict(
 bluf="Dred Scott declared that Black Americans could not be citizens and that Congress could not ban slavery in the territories. Meant to settle the slavery question, it made compromise impossible and hastened the Civil War.",
 lens="Court rulings that try to settle a deep political conflict can inflame it instead."),
"as185833": dict(
 bluf="The British Crown took direct control of India, replacing Company rule with a bureaucratic empire under a viceroy. The Raj built railways, courts and an English-educated elite while extracting revenue and presiding over devastating famines, a mixed legacy still fiercely debated.",
 then="British rule lasted 89 years. The English-educated middle class it created founded the Indian National Congress in 1885, which led the independence movement.",
 lens="Imperial education creates the elite that later demands independence."),
"jp-harris": dict(
 bluf="The 'unequal treaties' gave Western powers extraterritorial rights and control of Japan's tariffs, and were signed without the emperor's approval. Outrage turned anti-foreign anger against the shogunate and made revising the treaties the central goal of Meiji Japan.",
 lens="National humiliation can become the driving force of a modernization programme."),
"eu185973": dict(
 bluf="Darwin showed that species evolve through natural selection, explaining the diversity of life without divine design. The theory transformed biology and challenged religious accounts of creation; its misuse as 'social Darwinism' was used to justify racism and imperialism.",
 story="After over twenty years of research begun on the Beagle voyage, and prompted by Alfred Russel Wallace's independent discovery of the same idea, Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859. He argued that individuals vary, more are born than can survive, and those with helpful traits leave more offspring, so species change over long periods.",
 then="It set off fierce debates with religious leaders. Most scientists accepted evolution within decades, though natural selection itself was disputed until genetics confirmed it in the 1930s-40s. The 'survival of the fittest' was applied to societies and races, feeding eugenics and imperialist ideology.",
 lens="Scientific ideas get borrowed for political ends: separate what a theory says from what people claim it justifies."),
"as186034": dict(
 bluf="The burning of the Summer Palace by British and French troops became the defining symbol of Western humiliation of China. It forced the Qing to accept the Convention of Peking, which legalized opium and opened China further.",
 lens="Cultural destruction can leave deeper grievances than military defeat."),
"jp-sakurada": dict(
 bluf="The assassination of the shogun's chief minister showed that the shogunate could no longer protect its own leaders or control samurai anger over the treaties. It began a cycle of political violence that ended in the shogunate's fall.",
 lens="When a regime cannot protect its own top officials, its authority is collapsing."),
"d-oxforddebate": dict(
 bluf="The Oxford debate dramatized the clash between Darwinian science and religious authority and became a legend of science triumphing over dogma. The real exchange was more ambiguous, but it marked scientists' growing independence from the Church.",
 lens="Public debates matter more for the stories told about them than for what was actually said."),
"na186144": dict(
 bluf="The Civil War was fought over whether slavery would continue and whether states could leave the Union. The Union's victory abolished slavery and established the supremacy of the national government, but the collapse of Reconstruction left racial equality unfinished for another century.",
 story="After Abraham Lincoln, who opposed the spread of slavery, won the 1860 election without carrying a single Southern state, eleven Southern states seceded to protect slavery and formed the Confederacy. On April 12, 1861, Confederate forces fired on Fort Sumter in Charleston Harbor, and Lincoln called for troops to put down the rebellion.",
 why="Decades of conflict over slavery's expansion into the West, the South's dependence on the labor of about 4 million enslaved people, and the growing political power of the free-labor North.",
 trig="Lincoln's election in November 1860 and the Confederate attack on Fort Sumter in April 1861.",
 then="Some 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers died. The Union's industrial and manpower advantages, emancipation and Black enlistment eventually won. The 13th, 14th and 15th Amendments ended slavery, defined citizenship and protected voting rights, though Southern states later nullified many of those protections.",
 now="The war ended slavery and settled that the United States is one nation, not a club states can leave; arguments over Confederate monuments and the war's meaning continue.",
 lens="When a political system can no longer compromise over a core economic and moral question, secession or war follows."),
"eu186174": dict(
 bluf="Italy's unification combined Piedmont's diplomacy, Garibaldi's volunteer campaigns and French military help. It created a new great power, but the hasty merger of a richer north with a poorer south created a divide that persists.",
 lens="National unification often means one region absorbing the others on its own terms."),
"eu186174b": dict(
 bluf="Alexander II's emancipation of 23 million serfs was Russia's attempt to modernize after defeat in Crimea. It ended legal bondage but left peasants poor, indebted and hungry for land, feeding the unrest that culminated in revolution.",
 lens="Reforms from above that leave the core grievance unresolved can deepen instability."),
"la186223": dict(
 bluf="Napoleon III's invasion tried to set up a French-backed monarchy in Mexico while the United States was distracted by civil war. Mexican resistance, symbolized by the victory at Puebla, and later US pressure defeated the scheme and strengthened Mexican nationalism under Benito Juárez.",
 lens="Great powers exploit their rivals' distractions, and the window closes when the rival recovers."),
"na186345": dict(
 bluf="The Emancipation Proclamation turned the Union's war to preserve the nation into a war to end slavery. It let Black men enlist, kept Britain and France from recognizing the Confederacy, and set the course for permanent abolition in the 13th Amendment.",
 story="On January 1, 1863, using his war powers, Lincoln declared enslaved people in areas in rebellion 'forever free'. It did not apply to loyal slave states or to areas already under Union control, so it freed few people at once, but every Union advance now brought freedom, and Black men could join the Union army.",
 why="Lincoln wanted to weaken the Confederacy's labor force, recruit Black soldiers and make it politically impossible for Britain and France to back the South. Enslaved people fleeing to Union lines had already made slavery a war issue.",
 then="About 180,000 Black soldiers fought for the Union, Britain and France stayed out, and the 13th Amendment abolished slavery nationwide in 1865.",
 lens="Wars change their purposes: what begins as a fight to preserve a state can become a fight to transform it."),
"na186346": dict(
 bluf="Gettysburg ended Lee's last invasion of the North and, with Vicksburg's fall the next day, marked the turning point of the Civil War. Lincoln's address there redefined the war's purpose as equality and democracy.",
 lens="Words can define a war's meaning as much as battles decide its outcome."),
"oc-blackbird": dict(
 bluf="Blackbirding brought tens of thousands of Pacific Islanders to Queensland, many by deception or force, to work its sugar plantations. Their deportation under the White Australia policy showed how labor exploitation and racial exclusion went together.",
 lens="Societies import cheap labor and then exclude the laborers."),
"jp-kagoshima": dict(
 bluf="The British bombardment of Kagoshima convinced Satsuma, one of Japan's most anti-foreign domains, that it had to learn from the West rather than fight it. Satsuma and Chōshū went on to lead Meiji modernization.",
 lens="Defeat can turn the most resistant into the most effective reformers."),
"la186424": dict(
 bluf="The Paraguayan War was the deadliest in Latin American history, devastating Paraguay and confirming Brazil and Argentina as the region's powers. Paraguay lost much of its population and territory.",
 lens="A small state that takes on bigger neighbours at once risks annihilation."),
"na186547": dict(
 bluf="Lee's surrender ended the Confederacy's main army and effectively the Civil War. Grant's generous terms aimed at reconciliation, foreshadowing a peace that put reunion ahead of justice for the freed people.",
 lens="Generous surrender terms speed peace but can leave the losers' social order intact."),
"na186547b": dict(
 bluf="Lincoln's murder put Andrew Johnson, a Southern Democrat hostile to Black rights, in charge of Reconstruction. It tilted the peace toward leniency for the South and contributed to Reconstruction's failure.",
 lens="Assassinations change policy when the successor holds very different views."),
}
